  "raw_meta_yaml": "schema_version: 1\n\ncontext:\n  version: \"26.9.1\"\n\npackage:\n  name: yazi\n  version: ${{ version }}\n\nsource:\n  url: https://github.com/sxyazi/yazi/archive/v${{ version }}.tar.gz\n  sha256: 66857f1b670469daf258edd0bb2ea51d9ad3e2cab4eea9684028c80059fd6862\n\nbuild:\n  number: 1\n  script:\n    env:\n      CARGO_PROFILE_RELEASE_STRIP: symbols\n      CARGO_PROFILE_RELEASE_LTO: fat\n      VERGEN_GIT_SHA: v${{ version }}\n    content:\n      # cc-rs appends the generic ${CFLAGS} to every target it compiles for, so build\n      # scripts built for the build platform also get the target's -march/-mabi flags\n      # and fail (e.g. `x86_64-conda-linux-gnu-cc: unrecognized argument in option\n      # '-mabi=lp64d'` while compiling ring). The rust activation already mirrors these\n      # into the per-triple CFLAGS_<triple>/CXXFLAGS_<triple>/CPPFLAGS_<triple>\n      # variables, so the generic ones are redundant.\n      - if: linux and build_platform != target_platform\n        then:\n          - unset CFLAGS CXXFLAGS CPPFLAGS\n      # rustc links this target through `-fuse-ld=lld`, and rust-lld doesn't understand\n      # the ppc64le relocations GCC emits into jemalloc's objects (\"unknown relocation\n      # (31) against symbol _rjem_je_witness_prefork\"). GCC honours the last -fuse-ld,\n      # so appending this switches us back to GNU ld.\n      - if: target_platform == \"linux-ppc64le\"\n        then:\n          - export CARGO_BUILD_RUSTFLAGS=\"${CARGO_BUILD_RUSTFLAGS:-} -C link-arg=-fuse-ld=bfd\"\n      - if: unix\n        then:\n          - cargo install --path yazi-build --no-track --locked --bins yazi-build\n          - cargo install --path yazi-fm --no-track --locked --bins --root ${PREFIX} yazi-fm\n          - cargo install --path yazi-cli --no-track --locked --bins --root ${PREFIX} yazi-cli\n        else:\n          # `release-windows` keeps unwinding enabled, which yazi-fm requires on Windows\n          - cargo install --path yazi-build --no-track --locked --bins --profile release-windows yazi-build\n          - cargo install --path yazi-fm --no-track --locked --bins --root %LIBRARY_PREFIX% --profile release-windows yazi-fm\n          - cargo install --path yazi-cli --no-track --locked --bins --root %LIBRARY_PREFIX% --profile release-windows yazi-cli\n      - cargo-bundle-licenses --format yaml --output THIRDPARTY.yml\n\nrequirements:\n  build:\n    - ${{ stdlib(\"c\") }}\n    - ${{ compiler(\"rust\") }}\n    - ${{ compiler(\"c\") }}\n    - if: linux\n      then:\n        - make\n    - cargo-bundle-licenses\n  # Yazi runs fine on its own, but the default config shells out to a pile of\n  # external binaries, each of which unlocks one feature. `ya env` prints the\n  # list it probes for. These are exposed as CEP-44 optional dependency groups,\n  # so users opt in with e.g. `yazi[extras=[\"preview\", \"search\"]]`.\n  # Groups are trimmed on win-arm64 and linux-riscv64, where most of these\n  # tools aren't packaged for conda-forge yet.\n  extras:\n    # Everything the preset previewers and preloaders spawn. `file` is in here\n    # because MIME detection is what picks the previewer (and opener) at all.\n    preview:\n      - if: target_platform != \"win-arm64\" and target_platform != \"linux-riscv64\"\n        then:\n          - file # MIME detection, overridable via $YAZI_FILE_ONE\n          - ffmpeg # video thumbnails, plus ffprobe for media metadata\n          - imagemagick # `magick`, for formats image-rs can't decode\n          - poppler # `pdftoppm`, for PDF pages\n          - resvg # SVG rasterisation\n      - if: target_platform != \"linux-riscv64\"\n        then:\n          - 7zip # `7zz`, for archive previews and `extract.lua`\n      - if: unix\n        then:\n          - jq # JSON pretty-printing; no Windows build on conda-forge\n      - if: unix and target_platform != \"linux-riscv64\"\n        then:\n          - chafa # image fallback for terminals without a graphics protocol\n    # The default keymap binds `s` to `search --via=fd`, `S` to\n    # `search --via=rg`, `z` to the fzf plugin and `Z` to the zoxide plugin.\n    search:\n      - fd-find\n      - ripgrep\n      - fzf\n      - if: target_platform != \"win-arm64\" and target_platform != \"linux-ppc64le\" and target_platform != \"linux-riscv64\"\n        then:\n          - zoxide\n    # Yank/paste through the system clipboard. macOS uses the built-in `pbcopy`\n    # and Windows talks to the Win32 API directly, so this is X11-only; the\n    # Wayland path wants `wl-copy`, which conda-forge doesn't package.\n    clipboard:\n      - if: linux and target_platform != \"linux-riscv64\"\n        then:\n          - xclip\n          - xsel\n    # The \"Show media info\" and \"Show EXIF\" openers in the default config.\n    metadata:\n      - if: target_platform != \"win-arm64\" and target_platform != \"linux-riscv64\"\n        then:\n          - mediainfo\n      - if: unix and target_platform != \"linux-riscv64\"\n        then:\n          - exiftool\n    # `ya pkg add/upgrade` clones plugins and themes with git.\n    plugins:\n      - if: target_platform != \"win-arm64\"\n        then:\n          - git\n\ntests:\n  - script:\n      # Both binaries resolve the config/cache/state directories before parsing\n      # any argument. On Windows those come from the AppData known folders, which\n      # the shell resolves by expanding `%USERPROFILE%` out of\n      # `HKCU\\...\\Explorer\\User Shell Folders`. rattler-build only forwards\n      # SYSTEMROOT/WINDIR/COMSPEC/TEMP/TMP/PATHEXT into test scripts, so\n      # USERPROFILE is unset, `dirs::config_dir()` returns None and yazi panics\n      # with \"Failed to get config directory\". Hand it a throwaway profile —\n      # the directories have to exist because SHGetKnownFolderPath verifies them.\n      - if: win\n        then:\n          - mkdir \"%SRC_DIR%\\.home\\AppData\\Roaming\"\n          - mkdir \"%SRC_DIR%\\.home\\AppData\\Local\"\n          - set \"USERPROFILE=%SRC_DIR%\\.home\"\n      # `--help` triggers TTY setup before arg parsing in 26.9.1, which fails\n      # without a real tty; `--version` has a pre-parse fast path that avoids it\n      - yazi --version\n      - ya --version\n  - package_contents:\n      bin:\n        - yazi\n        - ya\n      strict: true\n\nabout:\n  homepage: https://github.com/sxyazi/yazi\n  summary: Blazing fast terminal file manager written in Rust, based on async I/O\n  description: |\n    Yazi (means \"duck\") is a terminal file manager written in Rust,\n    based on non-blocking async I/O. It aims to provide an efficient,\n    user-friendly, and customizable file management experience.\n  license: MIT\n  license_file:\n    - LICENSE\n    - LICENSE-ICONS\n    - THIRDPARTY.yml\n  documentation: https://yazi-rs.github.io/\n  repository: https://github.com/sxyazi/yazi\n\nextra:\n  recipe-maintainers:\n    - hoxbro\n    - pavelzw\n",
